# PROVENANCE. Section A is CAPTURED: every line is copied byte for byte out of
# the `log show --archive … --style syslog` exports uploaded by CI run
# 35280144455 (lanes e2e-ios-real-gps and e2e-ios-auth-tier), which is the run
# that proved the previous, specification-derived corpus wrong — the real
# rendering has two spaces before the host, a `(library)` column and an optional
# `[subsystem:category]` column, and no `<<Type>>` column at all. Each was read
# against the log-anonymity pillar before it was copied: they carry a boot
# daemon's own state, an Apple framework's own state, or a Haven line that
# names a migration and nothing else. No key, id, name, coordinate, URL, host,
# path or remote-authored string is in any of them.
#
# Section B is WRITTEN, because no capture can supply it: the ownership probes
# have to CARRY a structural shape to prove the rules ran (and did not run) on
# the right lines, and a real line carrying one would be a leak rather than a
# fixture. Its coordinate and pubkey are synthetic, its plant tokens are
# synthetic, and the `<<Type>>` and columnar renderings are the two the tool
# accepts besides the captured one.
#
# Ownership is the record's EMITTER: its subsystem, else its library, else its
# process. Haven's are `frb_user` (the Rust core's oslog subsystem), `haven_ios`
# (the subsystem every Haven Swift log call names), `rust_lib_haven` and
# `Runner` (Haven's own images) and `Flutter` (the engine's image, which carries
# Dart's `flutter: <msg>` output). The captured `(Flutter)` lines below are the
# three vendor shapes the real captures hold, and they are in Section A to prove
# that owning `Flutter` reddens none of them once the lane's loopback endpoint
# is exempt. The bare `Runner[pid]: <msg>` probe is the EMITTER FALLBACK and is
# WRITTEN for that reason: no capture has ever produced a Runner record without
# a library column, which is why the Swift plant's line below is the `(Runner)
# [haven_ios:runner]` shape an `os_log` call produces and not a bare one.
